{{smaller block|I warn the traveller who goes to see the lovely Madonnas of Bellini to beware how he trusts himself in winter to the gusty, ''arctic'' magnificence of the Church of the Redentore.
{{right|''Howells'', Venetian Life, iii.}}}}

{{smaller block|'''Arctic circle''', a small circle, parallel to the equator, distant from the north pole by an angular quantity equal to the obliquity of the ecliptic, or 23° 28′½. This and the ''antarctic'' circle are called the ''polar circles'', and within these lie the frigid zones, at every point within which the sun, theoretically, on at least one day in summer, passes through the north point without setting, and on at least one day in winter does not rise; practically, allowance must be made for the semidiameter and horizontal refraction of the sun.—'''Arctic fox''', a small species of fox, ''Vulpes lagopus'', of the family ''Canidæ'', celebrated for the}}
[[FIGURE]]
